    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Defined as the “precise co-occurrence of two behaviors in time”, synchrony plays a heavy hand in the lives of humans, who eat, sleep and walk together, and in the lives of animals too. Bees, wolves, and whales sleep, hunt, and forage synchronously. It is no secret that the earth is situated firmly in the middle of a mass extinction, and the disruption of collective animal behavior may be an unrecognized mechanism of accelerating biodiversity loss. But recent studies documenting population decline as a likely result of climate change-induced degradation of synchrony highlight the critical gap scientists must fill in their understanding of this phenomenon. This study assesses pair synchrony in blue whales (Balaenoptera musculus), a species that fails to return to pre-whaling numbers, and for which synchrony is almost entirely uncharacterized. Multi- sensor body-mounted tags were deployed on eight adult dyads, and subsequent analysis focused on the relationship between foraging activity and synchrony, among other important cofactors such as dive duration and depth. Synchrony was not unique to, but more prevalent in foraging dives. Among synchronous dives, foraging dives predicted an increase in temporal synchrony, but not increased spatial or directional synchrony, suggesting that synchrony may increase prey detection, but animals are not necessarily working together to hunt prey once located. Additionally, emergence of several patterns within the eight dyads suggests the existence of multiple “modes” of synchrony, each of which exhibit a unique suite of dive characteristics, and may offer slightly different advantages. Undoubtedly, the complex findings of this study insist on further exploration of blue whale synchrony, and point to the presence of different “synchronous behavioral modes or states” as a place to start. This study is the first comprehensive assessment of behavioral synchrony in foraging whale pairs and adds to our fundamental understanding of collective animal behavior. Such data will be instrumental in understanding the impacts of environmental change from individuals to populations.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
